<DocsPage id="desktop-app">

          <p>
            Agent and Editor are two modes over the same local repository and session, with repository-wide file search,
            diagnostics, a terminal, browser, review, and the same agent beside the file you opened.
          </p>

          <h2 id="agent-editor">Agent and Editor</h2>
          <p>
            Switch to Editor when you want to work directly: search the repository, open files in tabs, edit with language
            support, or keep the same agent beside the file and ask it to make the change.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Find a file</strong><span>Use repository search to open a file full-screen in a persistent tab.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Edit normally</strong><span>Your changes are written to the active repository or isolated workspace.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Ask beside the code</strong><span>The session does not restart when you switch views, so the agent keeps the task and its history.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            The session header can hand the repository to the editor or terminal you already use — Visual Studio Code,
            Sublime Text, TextMate, Android Studio, Antigravity, Ghostty or the system terminal — or copy the repository
            path. It also carries the toggles for the file tree, Changes and Terminal.
          </p>

          <h2 id="composer">The composer</h2>
          <p>
            The box at the bottom of a session is where you type the task, and it carries every per-message control: which agent answers, which model, how hard that model thinks, how fast to run (see <a href="/docs/vector-agent#modes">modes</a>), dictation, and attachments. It takes pasted images and dragged files, keeps a history of your prompts you can walk back through, and offers a popover of slash commands. Shell mode sends a command instead of a prompt, and it is refused while Plan Mode is on.
          </p>
          <p>
            For models that expose reasoning effort, the model popover shows a slider across that model's variants: <strong>Default</strong> follows the provider's recommendation, <strong>Light</strong> favours speed and cost on small tasks, <strong>Balanced</strong> trades speed against careful reasoning, and <strong>Max</strong> sits at the top of the range. The choice shows on the control, with its description in the tooltip.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Dictation is transcribed by a Whisper model that runs on your machine. The model file is fetched and cached
            the first time, after which nothing is sent to a cloud speech service.
          </p>

          <h2 id="transcript">The session transcript</h2>
          <p>
            The conversation is a timeline of turns: your messages, the agent's text, collapsible thinking blocks, tool calls with their status and error cards, and a per-turn summary of how many tools ran.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Comment on a line</strong><span>Click a line inside a code block or a diff in the conversation and attach a comment to it. The comment is carried into your next prompt as context, so "this branch is wrong" is tied to the exact line instead of described in prose. Annotations stay on the block until you send.</span></li>
            <li><strong>The todo dock</strong><span>While the agent works through a multi-step task, its plan sits as a live checklist above the composer, so the step in progress and the ones still outstanding are visible without scrolling the transcript.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="workspace-rail">The rail, the palette and search</h2>
          <p>
            The slim rail down the left edge is the app's top-level navigation: Home, Agents, Search, Agent Dashboard, Browser, Pull Requests, Plugins, Connections and Settings, with a help cluster for Docs, Best practices, Changelog, Diagnostics, Support and Send feedback.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Command palette</strong><span>Searches every action by category and runs it without the mouse, and some actions live only here, such as adding the current file selection to the prompt context. Shortcuts are editable in Settings → Keyboard shortcuts, which can also reset them to their defaults.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Chat search</strong><span>A search overlay finds sessions and messages across the workspace, so you can return to a conversation without scrolling the sidebar. It closes on Escape.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="terminal-review">Terminal and review</h2>
          <p>
            The Terminal runs commands inside the active repository or isolated workspace. Review keeps the files an
            agent changed, validation results, risk signals and merge controls together, so you can inspect what will
            land before it touches the primary checkout. See <a href="/docs/checkpoints#review">Review and recovery</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
          <p>
            Click your name at the bottom of the sidebar to open <strong>Settings</strong> at <strong>Account</strong>, or use the gear beside it, <strong>Settings</strong> in the Help menu, or Cmd+, (Ctrl+, on Windows and Linux). When an update has downloaded, an <strong>Update</strong> button takes the gear's place: it restarts Vector into the new version, and first asks whether to stop any agents in the workspace that are still running. From 1.99.109 Home shows the same <strong>Update</strong> button too, just before its <strong>Settings</strong> button.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Account</strong><span>Your Vector sign-in, your Vector Codium plan with the share of this month's credits left, and the way to delete your account. Desktop app only; signed out, it offers Sign in.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Notifications</strong><span>Desktop alerts, error alerts and sound effects.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Accessibility</strong><span>Interface scaling, high contrast, reduced motion, visible focus indicators and larger click targets.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong><span>The app's commands with their shortcuts, grouped as General, Session, Navigation, Model and agent, Terminal and Prompt, with a search by name or keys. Click a shortcut and press the new keys: Backspace or Delete clears it, Escape cancels, and keys another command already uses are refused with a notice naming that command. <strong>Reset to defaults</strong> puts every shortcut back.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Vector has one look, its own dark theme, and shows its interface in your computer's language. <strong>Vector Tab</strong>, the AI suggestions in the editor, is switched on and off from its label in the editor's status bar; the suggestions use the selected model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>General</strong> holds the <strong>Share usage counts</strong> switch, on by default. While the app is open it reports how many sessions and subagent sessions were active that day (in UTC) and the totals from <strong>Usage &amp; streaks</strong>: tokens by type, recorded cost, tokens and tasks per day, the models and providers you use with their share of tokens, effort levels, chats and streaks. It checks about once an hour and sends only when something has gone up. Each report carries a random install ID, your Vector account when you are signed in, the app version, operating system and CPU architecture, and never your prompts, code, file names or model output. Turn it off there to stop sending.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>General</strong> also has a <strong>Terminal</strong> card with the <strong>Terminal Shell</strong> picker, Auto by default: the shell the terminal and the composer's shell mode use. Agent tool calls use it too, unless the picker marks it terminal only, as it does fish.
          </p>

          <h2 id="remote-servers">Connect to a Vector server on another machine</h2>
          <p>
            The desktop app runs its own engine on your computer, and it can also work with a Vector server on another
            machine, such as a workstation that holds your repositories, started there with <code>vector serve</code>.
            Repositories you open on that server, and the provider keys, custom providers and MCP servers you add while
            working on it, are on that machine.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><span>1</span><div><strong>Start the server with a password</strong><p>On the other machine, set <code>VECTOR_SERVER_PASSWORD</code> from a protected secret source, and <code>VECTOR_SERVER_USERNAME</code> if you want a username other than <code>vector</code>. Then run <code>vector serve</code> with a <code>--port</code> and the <code>--hostname</code> to listen on. Read the rules below before it listens beyond loopback.</p></div></li>
            <li><span>2</span><div><strong>Add it in Settings</strong><p>In Settings → Environment &amp; servers, choose <strong>Add server</strong>. Enter the server address with its port, such as <code>http://192.168.1.20:4096</code>, an optional name, and under <strong>Authentication</strong> the username and password the server was started with. Vector checks the address as you type and adds the server only once it answers.</p></div></li>
            <li><span>3</span><div><strong>Work on it</strong><p>With more than one server, Home lists each one under <strong>Agents</strong> with its repositories, and <strong>Open repository</strong> beside a server lets you pick a folder on that machine. <strong>Set as default</strong> in a server's menu opens the app on that server the next time it starts, and <strong>Remove default</strong> goes back to the app's own engine.</p></div></li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Each server in the list shows whether it answers, its version and its username. Its menu also has
            <strong>Edit</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. An address typed without <code>http://</code> or
            <code>https://</code> uses <code>http://</code> for this computer or your own network (private, link-local
            and CGNAT addresses such as Tailscale's, and names such as <code>gpu-box</code> or
            <code>nas.local</code>) and <code>https://</code> for anything else. Vector refuses an address with a
            username or password inside it, and sends a password over plain HTTP only to this computer or your own
            network: <em>A password is sent over plain HTTP only to this computer or your own network. Use an https://
            address for this server.</em>
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            A server on your network is reachable by anyone who can reach that port. From 1.99.99 a server listening
            beyond loopback needs <code>VECTOR_SERVER_PASSWORD</code> unless you pass <code>--unsecured</code>; do not
            pass it on a shared network. From the next CLI release a server with a password also refuses to listen beyond
            loopback unless you pass <code>--trusted-network</code>, which is for a network you trust, such as a private LAN,
            because plain HTTP carries the password in every request. From anywhere else, keep the server on loopback
            behind a TLS reverse proxy or tunnel and add its <code>https://</code> address. Releases before 1.99.99 do
            not enforce the password rule, so keep them on loopback. See
            <a href="/docs/terminal-agent#cli-server">Server, web and editors</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="help-assistant">Help inside the app</h2>
          <p>
            The help panel answers questions without leaving Vector. Its <strong>Assistant</strong> tab replies only from Vector's own documentation, and says plainly when it cannot reach the service or has nothing to offer rather than inventing an answer; the <strong>Documentation</strong> tab browses the same material.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Send feedback</strong> in the same cluster files a bug report from inside the app, with an optional email address so the team can reply, and tells you when a report could not be delivered.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
